<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The hierarchy kept literal</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-splice-hierarchy">One, ten, one hundred—and the open role of one thousand</h2>
    <p class="lede">This assessment does not replace decimal splices with an arbitrary base. TTI’s existing
      Skeletal Splice argument treats base ten as the finding to be defended, not an unnoticed coordinate
      choice.</p>
    <p>The active proposal is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \pi\text{-level geometry}
      \longrightarrow
      10:\text{ localised gravitational source}
      \longrightarrow
      100:\text{ circular phase and electromagnetism}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The splice at one precedes these proposed physical promotions; whether it is a zeroth seed, the first
      Fibonacci cohort or a carrier-free boundary remains open. At the first post-seed splice, a deposit or
      scalar degree of freedom \(\chi\) is proposed to contribute stress&ndash;energy while the metric
      \(g_{\mu\nu}\), not \(\chi\), carries the observed massless spin-two mode. At the second post-seed
      splice, a normalised complex degree of freedom
      \(\Psi=R e^{i\theta}\) has phase space \(S^1\simeq U(1)\); local phase comparison motivates a
      connection \(A_\mu\) and covariant derivative \(D_\mu=\nabla_\mu+iqA_\mu\). Matter below the second
      threshold could then gravitate without carrying that electromagnetic phase, suggesting a dark sector.</p>
    <p>The Skeletal Splice already continues to \(1000\) and beyond. Under the existing post-seed convention,
      what remains open is whether that third post-seed threshold activates a genuinely new carrier—for example,
      a boundary residue that records the path through the first two faces—rather than another copy of
      localisation or phase.</p>
    <p>That architecture is conditional field theory. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> It is more disciplined
      than saying “ten is gravity and one hundred is light”, because it names the fields and symmetries that
      would have to exist. It still leaves four independent debts: select one action, introduce a physical
      scale, fix gauge and charge normalisation, and derive a universal radial anchor. The question here is
      whether the RH work pays any of them.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Third-splice test</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-third-splice">Can 1000 carry the missing arithmetic residue?</h2>
    <p>The existing rules already determine the third event. At \(n=3\), the threshold is \(10^3=1000\),
      the Fibonacci multiplicity is two, the number of returners is four, and</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \delta\Pi_3=4\varphi^{-3}=0.9442719\ldots.
    \]</p>
    <p>Summing every generation also gives the corrected exact asymptotic</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Pi_N=1.170820393\ldots N-1.570820393\ldots+o(1).
    \]</p>
    <p>Simply restricting the established \(\kappa_{\rm R}/m^2\) integer ledger to \(m=10^n\) leaves a
      smooth \(Ct^2\) kernel and cannot match the Riemann screw cusp. A more substantive third-splice model
      treats its residue as the boundary value of a harmonic field in one additional dimension. Solving that
      declared action gives the Dirichlet-to-Neumann covariance</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      G_{\partial}(\lambda)=\frac{\kappa_{\rm R}}{|\lambda|}.
    \]</p>
    <p>With literal decimal frequencies \(\lambda_n=10^n\) and accumulated strength \(\Pi_n\), its weights
      are \(w_n=\kappa_{\rm R}\Pi_n/10^n\). This positive kernel has the significant asymptotic</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      D_{\partial}(t,t)=\Theta\!\left(t\log\frac1t\right),
    \]</p>
    <p>so the third-splice boundary mechanism escapes the earlier quadratic no-go. It does not equal the Weil
      kernel. A discrete decimal tower leaves a nonconstant log-periodic coefficient. Filling every decade
      continuously removes that oscillation but gives coefficient \(0.0175731\ldots\), not the required unit
      coefficient; the already-derived \(\kappa_{\rm R}\) has not been retuned.</p>
    <p>There is also an arithmetic support mismatch. Decimal events occur at \(n\log10\), whereas the exact
      screw potential has jumps at every \(\log(p^k)\). Möbius extraction recovers those events only from the
      full all-integer ledger, and its delayed-hinge transform is not positivity-preserving. Thus \(1000\)
      supplies a coherent candidate for the <em>regularity class</em> of a history channel, not an independently
      derived Weil identity. The full calculation is retained in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-third-splice-weil-channel.md</code>. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Alternative residue channels</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-alternative-channels">The gamma factor fixes what decimal scaling could not</h2>
    <p>The unconditional Riemann&ndash;von Mangoldt mean density leads to the positive spectral measure</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \rho_\Gamma(\lambda)
      =\frac{\log(\lambda/2\pi)}{\pi\lambda^2},
      \qquad\lambda\ge2\pi.
    \]</p>
    <p>Its Gram diagonal satisfies</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      D_\Gamma(t,t)=t\log(1/t)+O(t)
    \]</p>
    <p>with the exact unit coefficient. It is non-lacunary, independently PSD and much closer locally to
      Suzuki&rsquo;s no-prime diagonal than the decimal boundary tower. It remains a continuous mean model and
      does not contain the fluctuating prime/zero remainder.</p>
    <p>A stronger arithmetic proxy projects every non-trivial zero \(\rho=\beta+i\gamma\) to its real
      ordinate \(\gamma\), retaining multiplicity and weight \(2/\gamma^2\). This channel is unconditionally
      PSD even if \(\beta\ne1/2\), because ordinates are real. It equals the screw kernel under RH; conversely,
      equality would imply RH. The discarded horizontal correction changes sign, so it cannot be added back as
      one positive feature per off-line zero.</p>
    <p>A positive prime-power sampling channel can also reproduce the gamma mean asymptotically, but it uses
      different weights and turns event times into frequencies. Direct P&oacute;lya-kernel features remain
      quadratic at the origin. The exact explicit-formula channel retains all arithmetic information but is
      signed. The best remaining target is therefore a positive completion of the fluctuation between the gamma
      mean and the exact prime/zero distribution. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-alternative-weil-channels.md</code>. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Two-inversion ordinate splice</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-ordinate-splice">The discarded horizontal coordinate is an exact residue</h2>
    <p>Center a non-trivial zero at the critical line:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      z=\rho-\frac12=\eta+i\gamma.
    \]</p>
    <p>The completed zeta function supplies two different involutions. Its functional equation sends
      \(z\mapsto-z\); conjugation sends \(z\mapsto\overline z\). Applying both gives the upper-half-plane
      reflection \(S:z\mapsto-\overline z\), or \((\eta,\gamma)\mapsto(-\eta,\gamma)\). The two exact
      projectors are therefore</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      Q_{\rm ord}=\frac{1+S}{2},\qquad
      Q_{\rm hor}=\frac{1-S}{2},\qquad
      Q_{\rm ord}z=i\gamma,\quad Q_{\rm hor}z=\eta.
    \]</p>
    <p>This makes the ordinate projection a literal two-channel sum-and-difference splice. For the two upper
      members \(z_+=\eta+i\gamma\) and \(z_-=-\eta+i\gamma\), the normalized Hadamard transform gives</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      q=\frac{z_++z_-}{\sqrt2}=\sqrt2\,i\gamma,
      \qquad
      r=\frac{z_+-z_-}{\sqrt2}=\sqrt2\,\eta.
    \]</p>
    <p>The pair count is the same two appearing in TTI&rsquo;s third-splice multiplicity \(F_3=2\). That is a
      structural match, not a derivation of zeta quartets from the decimal threshold. It does, however, give
      precise mathematical content to a returned phase channel accompanied by a history-bearing residue.</p>
    <p>Most of the exact correction produced by \(\eta\) changes sign, so it cannot be added to the Weil
      kernel as one positive feature per zero. At the central seam one positive quantity survives:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      H_{\rm ord}'(0)-H'(0)
      =\sum_{\text{off-line quartets}}
      \frac{4\eta^2(\eta^2+3\gamma^2)}
      {\gamma^2(\eta^2+\gamma^2)^2}
      =\lVert B_{\rm hor}\rVert^2\ge0.
    \]</p>
    <p>This horizontal-residue norm vanishes exactly when every \(\eta\) vanishes, which is equivalent to RH.
      Its positivity is unconditional; proving that its value is zero remains the whole difficulty.</p>
    <p>Arithmetic Möbius inversion is not the same reflection. The exact chain is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \log n\xrightarrow{\,\mu *\,}\Lambda(n)
      \xrightarrow{\text{completion}}\Psi
      \xrightarrow{\mathcal L}\frac{\xi'}{\xi}
      \longleftrightarrow\{\text{zero quartets}\}
      \xrightarrow{S}(\gamma,\eta).
    \]</p>
    <p>Divisor summation reverses the arithmetic extraction and restores the all-integer ledger; it does not
      force \(\eta=0\). The newly isolated target is to derive the vanishing of the positive zero-side residue
      from that completed primitive ledger. The exact calculation and its signed full-domain limitation are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-ordinate-projection-splice-mobius.md</code>. No RH proof is claimed.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Third-density bounce test</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-third-density-bounce">The missing one creates drift, not contraction</h2>
    <p>The stations 333 and 666 really are a reflection pair. Normalising by the last full point 999 sends
      them to \(1/3\) and \(2/3\), and the complement \(n\mapsto999-n\) becomes the same centered operation
      \(\eta\mapsto-\eta\) seen in the xi zero plane.</p>
    <p>If 999 is identified with the seam at zero, translation by 333 gives an exact order-three orbit and a
      \(D_3\) reflection system. Retaining the actual carry into 1000 changes the result:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      T(n)=n+333\pmod{1000},\qquad
      T^3(n)=n-1\pmod{1000}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The displayed formula uses the ascending direction; the descending
      \(999\to666\to333\) route has \(T_-^3(n)=n+1\). In either direction, because 333 is coprime to 1000, this orbit visits all 1000 residue states before returning. The missing
      one in \(3\cdot333=1000-1\) is an exact one-step drift after each three moves. Translation and complement
      generate a finite dihedral permutation; their Fourier eigenvalues have modulus one. The bounce is
      therefore periodic and norm-preserving, not damping.</p>
    <p>The initial route can be re-read as a different affine construction:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      999\xrightarrow{\text{contract to one third}}666
      \xrightarrow{\text{reflect}}333,
      \qquad
      \eta\mapsto-\frac{\eta}{3}.
    \]</p>
    <p>But iteration then gives \(333\to444\to555\), leaving the named three stations. The factor-one-third
      contraction is a valid new rule, not a consequence of the exact bounce arithmetic.</p>
    <p>A strict contraction would be decisive if it preserved xi zeros. The bounce-contraction lemma says that
      for any \(0&lt;a&lt;1\),</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \xi\!\left(\frac12+\eta+i\gamma\right)=0
      \Longrightarrow
      \xi\!\left(\frac12-a\eta+i\gamma\right)=0
      \quad\Longrightarrow\quad\mathrm{RH}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Otherwise repeated images would be distinct zeros accumulating at the finite point
      \(1/2+i\gamma\), impossible for a nonzero entire function. Conversely, RH makes every such contraction
      fix every zero. The proposed zero-set symmetry is therefore RH-equivalent, not an independent premise.</p>
    <p>Direct completed-xi diagnostics also reject translation as a spectral symmetry: shifting the first zero
      from real part \(1/2\) to \(5/6\) gives \(|\xi|\approx4.63\times10^{-4}\), rather than a zero residual.
      Equal bouncing between opposite sides only cancels \(\eta\) linearly; it cannot cancel the positive
      horizontal norm built from \(\eta^2\).</p>
    <p>The full arithmetic and xi tests are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-third-density-bounce.md</code>. They leave one precise open route: derive a strict
      horizontal zero contraction from the completed von Mangoldt ledger. The 999&ndash;666&ndash;333 arithmetic
      alone does not do so. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Splice-at-one correction</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-golden-catchup">The delayed history begins at one</h2>
    <p>The threshold sequence is \(1,10,100,1000,\ldots\), and every splice has its own clock. At decimal
      level \(\ell\), let \(d_\ell\) be its initial golden-depth index and \(k_\ell\) its local clock:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      g_{\ell,k_\ell}=\varphi^{-(d_\ell+k_\ell)},
      \qquad a_{\ell,k_\ell}=1-g_{\ell,k_\ell}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Including the splice at \(1\) leaves three coherent indexing choices:</p>
    <ul class="biblio">
      <li><strong>Zeroth seed:</strong> one seed carrier at \(1\), while \(10,100,1000\) retain
        \(F_1,F_2,F_3\). There are five carriers through 1000.</li>
      <li><strong>Reindexed Fibonacci:</strong> \(1,10,100,1000\) receive
        \(F_1,F_2,F_3,F_4\). There are seven carriers, but all later splice labels shift.</li>
      <li><strong>Boundary only:</strong> \(1\) carries a delay but no theta carrier. The existing four
        carriers through 1000 remain; without a coupling, this boundary cannot alter the zeros.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For any selected model, give each carrier-bearing cohort a consecutive theta block \(B_\ell\) and set</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Psi_{N,\mathbf k}(x)=
      \sum_{\ell}\left(1-\varphi^{-(d_\ell+k_\ell)}\right)
      \sum_{m\in B_\ell}e^{-\pi m^2x},
    \]</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Xi_{N,\mathbf k}(s)=\frac12+
      \frac{s(s-1)}2\int_1^\infty\Psi_{N,\mathbf k}(x)
      \left(x^{s/2}+x^{(1-s)/2}\right)\frac{dx}{x}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Every finite member is entire, obeys both xi symmetries and converges locally uniformly to \(\xi\)
      along cofinal clock schedules. All three indexings were tested with chronological clocks and with equal
      local ticks. At cutoffs 14, 20, 30 and 40, all six paths had total zero counts 2, 2, 4 and 3; every
      total matched its critical-line count. The reindexed model moved the finite ordinates, but no off-line
      zero was detected through height 30.</p>
    <p>This is bounded-window evidence. Every model still admits the escaping quartet</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      s=\frac12\pm\frac14\pm i\varphi^N,
    \]</p>
    <p>through an even real factor tending to one on compact sets. The splice at \(1\) corrects the finite
      history but does not control zeros whose heights grow with the cutoff. TTI must still choose the indexing,
      derive the clocks and provide uniform-in-height zero accounting. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-golden-catchup-completions.md</code>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Internal third-density splice test</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-internal-splices">What if 333, 666 and 999 are splices too?</h2>
    <p>Their positions make them plausible internal gates:
      \(333=999/3\), \(666=2\cdot999/3\), and complement reflection exchanges 333 with 666. But promoting
      a distinguished station to a splice does not yet say what it does to population. Three readings were
      tested.</p>
    <ul class="biblio">
      <li><strong>One new carrier each:</strong> retain the decimal Fibonacci cohorts and add one independently
        delayed carrier at 333, 666 and 999. Depending on the splice-at-one convention, there are 8, 10 or 7
        carriers through 1000.</li>
      <li><strong>Fibonacci at every event:</strong> assign \(F_1,\ldots,F_7\) to
        \(1,10,100,333,666,999,1000\). <strong>Thirty-three carriers</strong> then exist through 1000, which
        becomes the seventh event rather than the third or fourth.</li>
      <li><strong>Shared faces:</strong> treat the stations as three delayed faces of the existing 1000 cohort.
        Its theta coefficient uses the mean of their three admitted fractions, so no population is added.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For decimal-scale models, the internal depth coordinate was interpolated as
      \(d(q)=\log_{10}q\), shifted by one under the reindexed splice-at-one convention. This is a declared
      analytic model, not a previously derived TTI law. The shared-face reading has one notable fit: only the
      reindexed model gives \(F_4=3\) carriers at 1000, allowing one carrier for each face without averaging.</p>
    <p>Seven compatible population scenarios were crossed with chronological and equal-local-ticks schedules.
      All fourteen paths again gave zero counts 2, 2, 4 and 3 through height 30 at cutoffs 14, 20, 30 and 40.
      Every total matched its critical-line roots. The all-event Fibonacci model moved the first finite roots
      most strongly, but no off-line zero was detected in the bounded rectangle.</p>
    <p>The global conclusion does not change. An escaping quartet with fixed horizontal displacement can be
      added while retaining the completion symmetries and compact xi limit. Extra splices change carrier counts,
      clocks and root ordinates; they do not supply uniform-in-height zero control. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-third-density-splice-events.md</code>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
