<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Repeated-halving test</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-splice-one-halving">What if every splice at 1 halves everything else?</h2>
    <p>Three readings must be separated: horizontal zero displacement can halve, downstream population or
      admitted weights can halve, or the remaining interval to full admission can halve. Only the first has
      an immediate RH mechanism.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      H(\beta+i\gamma)
      =\frac12+\frac{\beta-1/2}{2}+i\gamma,
      \qquad
      H^n(\beta+i\gamma)
      =\frac12+2^{-n}(\beta-\tfrac12)+i\gamma.
    \]</p>
    <p><strong>Same-xi halving</strong> would prove RH: if every iterate were another zero of the same entire
      function \(\xi\), an off-line zero would generate infinitely many distinct zeros accumulating at the
      finite point \(1/2+i\gamma\). The identity theorem forbids that. Conversely RH makes every zero a fixed
      point, so this zero-preserving halving property is RH-equivalent rather than a separately derived proof.</p>
    <p>Literal halving of admitted theta weights drives those contributions to zero and loses the xi limit.
      Literal population halving is undefined for odd Fibonacci cohorts without another rule. The coherent
      carrier reading is instead to halve the <em>remaining deficit</em>:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      g_{n+1}=\frac{g_n}{2},
      \qquad
      a_{n+1}=1-\frac{g_n}{2}=\frac{1+a_n}{2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>This is exactly remaining-interval halving. It accelerates downstream carriers toward full admission,
      preserves both completion symmetries and retains local convergence to \(\xi\). Six finite diagnostic
      paths had no detected off-line zero through height 30; the boundary-only roots rapidly approached the
      first xi ordinates.</p>
    <p>The carrier result does not imply horizontal zero contraction. At finite stages, inherited zeros can
      halve while a new quartet appears at height \(\varphi^N\). A proof requires either a derivation that
      \(H\) preserves the same xi zero set or a global \(2^{-N}\) strip containing every zero of every finite
      completion. Full details are in <code>docs/rh/rss-splice-at-one-halving.md</code>.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Obstruction-closing attempt</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-halving-obstruction">Can the halving obstruction be closed?</h2>
    <p>Pointwise same-xi invariance can be weakened. The ordinate splice already supplies a convergent positive
      residue \(\mathcal R\) that vanishes exactly under RH. One-to-one horizontal halving strictly contracts
      every nonzero summand. If splice closure could be shown to return the same aggregate residue, then</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal R_{\rm after}=\mathcal R_{\rm before},
      \qquad
      0\le\mathcal R_{\rm after}\lt\mathcal R_{\rm before}
      \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
      \mathcal R=0.
    \]</p>
    <p>This <strong>aggregate fixed-point</strong> theorem would prove RH without mapping each zero to another
      xi zero. The missing bridge is a derivation of residue conservation from the completed prime ledger or
      the infinity seam.</p>
    <p>A second sufficient route is a <strong>complete zero genealogy</strong>: every finite-stage zero has a
      parent, each child halves its parent displacement and no unparented zero is born at any height. That gives
      a global \(2^{-N-1}\) strip and Rouché–Hurwitz transfer to RH. The nonmonotone bounded counts do not yet
      provide such a genealogy.</p>
    <p>Branch multiplicity adds a warning. Four spectral descendants at half displacement satisfy</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      4(\eta/2)^2=\eta^2.
    \]</p>
    <p>Thus the four-way carrier fork at 1000 cannot also be assumed to create four zero quartets: it would
