    <p><strong>Canonical interlacing did not.</strong> On the positive spectral axis, the \(N=14\) and \(N=20\)
      deficit-halving zero lists contain adjacent roots from the same stage. The current refinements therefore do not form a
      strict interlacing chain. A new carrier-sized Sturm or Hermite&ndash;Biehler refinement would have to be
      constructed rather than inferred from positive kernel addition.</p>
    <p><strong>A Blaschke seam divisor was constructed.</strong> Put</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      q=e^{i\pi(s-1/2)},
      \qquad
      w=\frac{q-1}{q+1}.
    \]</p>
    <p>This maps the critical strip to the disk, sends infinite height to \(w=-1\) or \(w=+1\), and maps the
      critical line to the real diameter. The zero count and exponential radial decay imply
      \(\sum_\rho(1-|w_\rho|)&lt;\infty\), so every zero entering from infinity belongs to a convergent
      Blaschke divisor.</p>
