<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Arithmetic seam control</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-arithmetic-seam-control">Can primes control the two-ended seam?</h2>
    <p>An exact controller can be written, although its decisive bound remains open. Use the generalized Li map</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      z_a(s)=\frac{s+a-1}{s-a},
      \qquad a=-1,
    \]</p>
    <p>which is based at \(s=2\). It maps the critical line to the unit circle and maps one member of every
      hypothetical off-line quartet inside that circle. Applying it to the endpoint-completed prime function
      \((s-1)\zeta(s)\) gives coefficients</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      p_n=1+(-1)^{n+1}2^n
      -3\sum_{m\ge2}\frac{\Lambda(m)}{m^2}
      L_{n-1}^{(1)}(3\log m).
    \]</p>
    <p>For each fixed \(n\), this von Mangoldt&ndash;Laguerre series converges absolutely. Its generating
      function has radius one exactly when no nontrivial zero maps inside the disk. Therefore</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathrm{RH}
      \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
      \limsup_{n\to\infty}|p_n|^{1/n}=1.
    \]</p>
    <p>The needed arithmetic theorem is the subexponential cancellation \(p_n=e^{o(n)}\). This would force
      the two-ended seam infimum to one. It has not been proved: fixed-index absolute convergence does not
      control the sequence uniformly as \(n\to\infty\).</p>
    <p>An escaping quartet is not lost, but it can require an enormous coefficient index. For displacement
      \(1/4\) at height \(\varphi^{14}\), the fixed \(s=2\) controller has an e-fold detection scale near
      947,532; the scale grows quadratically with height. Thus the infinite family repairs the seam’s
      height-decay problem without turning any finite prefix into a proof. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-arithmetic-seam-control.md</code>. No proof of RH is obtained.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
