</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Prime&ndash;Laguerre proof attempt</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-prime-laguerre-growth-proof">Was the subexponential prime–Laguerre bound proved?</h2>
    <p>No. The large endpoint term can be removed exactly, but the remaining estimate is a square-root prime-error
      problem. If \(f_n(x)=x^{-2}L_{n-1}^{(1)}(3\log x)\), then</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      3\int_1^\infty f_n(x)\,dx
      =1-(-2)^n,
    \]</p>
    <p>which is precisely the endpoint term in \(p_n\). With
      \(\widetilde E(x)=\psi(x)-(x-1)\), the completed remainder is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      p_n=-3\int_1^\infty f_n(x)\,d\widetilde E(x).
    \]</p>
    <p>Thus the exponential endpoint is not an unexplained reservoir: it is the transform of the continuous
      density \(dx\). The unresolved part is a uniform signed Laguerre transform of the Chebyshev prime error.</p>
    <p>Termwise absolute values destroy the cancellation. Positivity is unavailable because
      \(L_{n-1}^{(1)}\) has \(n-1\) positive roots and alternates sign. Ordinary prime-number-theorem errors
      remain much larger than the square-root scale \(\psi(x)-x=O(x^{1/2}\log^2x)\) that would close the bound.
      Verification to any finite height only makes a possible exponential rate small, not zero.</p>
    <p>The target \(p_n=e^{o(n)}\) therefore remains unproved and RH-equivalent. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-prime-laguerre-growth-proof.md</code>. No proof of RH is obtained.
