      \qquad
      B_\theta=\frac{1+\theta}{2-\theta}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Only \(\theta=1/2\) gives \(B_\theta=1\). Every fixed loss above the square-root exponent remains
      exponential in the coefficient index.</p>
    <p>The explicit formula shows why route A stops: a zero with \(\Re\rho=\beta&gt;1/2\) contributes at
      \(x^\beta\) scale. Route B stops at the same point: Laguerre orthogonality requires a critical weighted
      norm of the Chebyshev error, while recurrence introduces uncontrolled logarithmic prime moments. Available
      zero-density and averaged-in-height estimates permit an exceptional zero, but one exception changes the
      seam infimum and the generating radius.</p>
    <p>Thus square-root prime error, subexponential Laguerre cancellation and zero-freeness in
      \(\Re s&gt;1/2\) are equivalent boundaries. Neither attempt derives the missing arithmetic inequality.
      Full details are in <code>docs/rh/rss-dual-prime-error-routes.md</code>. RH remains open.
