<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Critical Chebyshev energy</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-critical-chebyshev-energy">What is the missing theorem?</h2>
    <p>It can be stated more weakly than pointwise square-root prime error. The exact positive target is the
      all-scale dyadic estimate</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \forall\delta&gt;0,
      \qquad
      \int_X^{2X}|\psi(x)-x|^2\,dx
      \ll_\delta X^{2+\delta}.
    \]</p>
    <p>This allows errors above square-root scale on sparse sets. Even so, it excludes every exceptional zero.
      With \(\widetilde E(x)=\psi(x)-(x-1)\), the estimate is equivalent to finiteness, for every
      \(\varepsilon&gt;0\), of</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      I_\varepsilon
      =\int_1^\infty
      |\widetilde E(x)|^2x^{-2-2\varepsilon}\,dx.
    \]</p>
    <p>Cauchy&ndash;Schwarz then continues the Mellin transform
      \(-s^{-1}\,d\log((s-1)\zeta(s))/ds\) throughout \(\Re s&gt;1/2\). A zero there would make the same
      function singular, so the critical energy theorem implies RH. RH conversely supplies the theorem via the
      standard \(O(x^{1/2}\log^2x)\) error estimate.</p>
    <p>The energy also has an exact finite von Mangoldt pair expansion. For the Laguerre route, its weighted
      norms combine with</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \sum_{n\ge1}
      \left[2L_{n-1}^{(1)}(t)+3L_{n-2}^{(2)}(t)\right]z^n
      =\frac{z(2+z)}{(1-z)^3}
      \exp\!\left(-\frac{tz}{1-z}\right).
    \]</p>
    <p>The resulting Hilbert-space radius reproduces exactly the previous coefficient base and tends to one as
      \(\varepsilon\to0\), proving that the energy estimate would imply \(p_n=e^{o(n)}\).</p>
    <p>The theorem has therefore been isolated in a positive form that is weaker than pointwise control, but its
      all-\(X\) estimate has not been proved. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-critical-chebyshev-energy.md</code>. RH remains open.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
