      every finite \(J\), choose</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac1{2(J+1)}&lt;\eta_J&lt;\frac1{2J},
      \qquad
      Q_{\eta_J}=\frac12(p_{\eta_J}+p_{-\eta_J}).
    \]</p>
    <p>Then \(Q_{\eta_J}\) has finite \(A_1,\ldots,A_J\) but divergent \(A_{J+1}\). It retains positivity,
      normalization, reflection, logarithmic dephasing, finite entropy, all fixed log moments, Fisher flow,
      phase invariance, trace class and strict convexity. Thus none of those properties, separately or together,
      crosses the next convergence boundary.</p>
    <p>This does not rule out a new arithmetic identity special to the actual \(\xi\) divisor. It rules out
      repeating finite structural recombinations as though they supplied that identity.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> all one hundred finite-bootstrap routes have exact separators. No new zero-free
      strip was proved. Full details are in <code>docs/rh/rss-hundred-round-mellin-closure-search.md</code>.
      RH remains open. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The prime-pair catch-up at 101</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-prime-pair-splice-link">Does the \((101,101)\) prime term close the same-\(\xi\) contraction link?</h2>
    <p>It is a genuine prime-power event: \(\Lambda(101)=\log101\). In the finite Chebyshev pair ledger,
      the diagonal term is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Lambda(101)^2[2X-\max(X,101)]_+
      =X(\log101)^2\quad(X\ge101).
    \]</p>
    <p>But this is one local \(O(X)\) term, not the all-scale cancellation required of the complete pair energy.
      In the centered Fisher curvature that generates the prime-family flow, the \((101,101)\) self-pair
      vanishes exactly because its factor is \(\log^2(101/101)=0\); only cross-pairs of distinct prime powers
      contribute to the variance.</p>
    <p>The exact arithmetic-to-zero route is the critical Chebyshev-energy criterion: if
      \(\int_X^{2X}|\psi(x)-x|^2dx\ll_\delta X^{2+\delta}\) for every \(\delta&gt;0\) and all \(X\),
      its Mellin transform is holomorphic in \(\Re s&gt;1/2\), excluding every zero to the right of the
      critical line. But that all-scale bound is equivalent to RH and has not been proved.</p>
    <p>The one-point prime-anchor defect also gives an exact positive sum over horizontal zero displacements.
      Its certified upper bound is below \(9.274\times10^{-37}\), not zero. No prime identity yet makes the
      actual \(\xi\)-zero ledger invariant under the derived contraction.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> the prime-to-zero criterion is explicit; the local \((101,101)\) event does not
      supply its all-scale hypothesis or same-\(\xi\) closure. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-prime-pair-splice-link.md</code>. RH remains open. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Test of a prime-side channel</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-prime-channel-no-go">Can one fixed stochastic prime operation implement golden contraction?</h2>
    <p>No. Suppose one parameter-independent Markov kernel sent every von-Mangoldt state \(p_a\) in an
      open interval to \(p_{2+c(a-2)}\), with \(0&lt;c&lt;1\). Analytic continuation would extend that identity
      to all \(a&gt;1\). As \(a\to\infty\), both input and target concentrate at prime power 2, forcing the
      channel to leave 2 fixed.</p>
    <p>But the contracted target then needs more mass at prime 3 than exists in the entire input tail outside 2:
      their leading ratio grows like \((3/2)^{(1-c)(a-2)}\). This contradicts positivity and mass conservation.
      It rules out a natural, parameter-independent channel—including \(c=1/\varphi\)—as the missing link.</p>
    <p>This does not rule out a nonlinear same-\(\xi\) identity or a rule defined only on the actual discrete
      zero set. It does not establish that the actual zero ledger follows the feature-family flow.
      <strong>Answer:</strong> one candidate prime-side bridge is now ruled out exactly; the same-\(\xi\)
      arithmetic link remains open. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-prime-channel-contraction-no-go.md</code>. RH remains open.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Nonlinear prime-ledger interpolation</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-geometric-prime-reweighting">Can a nonlinear prime map realize the contraction?</h2>
    <p>Yes, on the prime-probability family. For a probability ledger \(q\), let</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal G_c(q)(n)=
      \frac{q(n)^c p_0(n)^{1-c}}
      {\sum_m q(m)^c p_0(m)^{1-c}}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Then \(\mathcal G_c(p_\eta)=p_{c\eta}\) exactly, including the golden choice \(c=1/\varphi\).
      Its fixed points are \(p_0\) conditioned on their support, so \(p_0\) is the unique full-support
      fixed point. Relative entropy strictly decreases for every full-support input other than \(p_0\).
      The actual zero mixture is full-support because each feature gives positive mass to every prime power.</p>
    <p>Therefore, if an independent same-\(\xi\) theorem showed
      \(\mathcal G_c(P_\xi)=P_\xi\), it would force \(P_\xi=p_0\) and RH. But no explicit-formula identity
      establishes that invariance; it is already RH-equivalent. This construction bypasses the fixed Markov
      channel no-go, not the same-\(\xi\) arithmetic gap.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> an exact nonlinear prime-side contraction exists; its application to the actual
      zero mixture remains unproved. Full details are in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-geometric-prime-reweighting.md</code>. RH remains open.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Local Euler-factor test</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-local-prime-power-diagonal">Can one prime-power tower detect any off-line zero?</h2>
    <p>For a fixed prime \(p\), define \(a_k=P_\xi(p^k)/\log p\). The positive reflected zero mixture makes
      \(a_k\) a moment sequence in the rates \(p^{-2+2\eta_\rho}\) and \(p^{-2-2\eta_\rho}\). Hence</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      a_k a_{k+2}-a_{k+1}^2
      =\frac12\iint (rs)^k(r-s)^2\,d\nu_p(r)d\nu_p(s)\ge0.
    \]</p>
    <p>Under RH the tower is geometric, so this determinant is zero. Conversely, one exact zero determinant
      for one prime would force the positive rate measure to be a point mass; reflected off-line features
      contribute two distinct rates, so RH would follow.</p>
    <p><strong>But the determinant for the actual \(P_\xi\) has not been computed from prime data.</strong>
      The ordinary explicit formula is linear in one zero variable; this local statistic depends on
      \(\rho+\overline\rho\) and requires a diagonal zero-pair identity. No such identity is proved here.
      This is a sharp RH-equivalent test, not the \((101,101)\) raw-energy term and not the missing link.
      Details: <code>docs/rh/rss-local-prime-power-diagonal-link.md</code>. RH remains open.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Gate two</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-gravity-scale">No physical scale for \(G\)</h2>
    <p>Newton’s constant is dimensionful. A sequence of pure numbers, a zeta ordinate, a prime logarithm or a
      positive matrix cannot determine it until a physical unit enters. In a first-splice effective theory one
      may write schematically</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      S_{10}=\int d^4x\sqrt{-g}\left[
        \frac{c^3}{16\pi G}R
        -\frac12(\nabla\chi)^2-V_{10}(\chi)
      \right].
    \]</p>
    <p>Dimensional analysis then permits an expression such as</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      G=C_{10}\frac{c^3\ell_{10}^2}{\hbar}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The splice arithmetic might eventually determine a dimensionless \(C_{10}\), but it cannot produce the
      length \(\ell_{10}\) from no physical input. Nor does the RH zero spectrum help merely by containing
      numbers with a natural order: its ordinates are dimensionless in the standard normalisation. Assigning
      metres, seconds or joules to one of them is a new physical postulate.</p>
    <p>The archive’s shrinking margins sharpen this point. A small dimensionless eigenvalue is not a small
      mass, cosmological constant or coupling until an action fixes how the eigenvalue enters a propagator and
      a scale converts it. Numerical proximity to \(G\), the Planck scale or a galactic scale after unit choices
      would be fitting, not derivation.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> no physical scale appears. The open requirement for \(\ell_{10}\) survives
      unchanged. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Gate three</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-charge-normalisation">No unique \(e\) or \(\alpha_{\rm EM}\)</h2>
    <p>A second-splice phase can motivate the topology of electromagnetism. If \(\Psi=R e^{i\theta}\) and
      phase comparison is local, then</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      D_\mu\Psi=(\nabla_\mu+iqA_\mu)\Psi,
      \qquad
      F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu.
    \]</p>
    <p>Exact unbroken local \(U(1)\) would forbid a bare Proca mass, and an anomaly-free current could be
      conserved. Opposite winding orientations can represent opposite signs of charge. These are conditional
      structural consequences, not yet the observed charge spectrum.</p>
    <p>The normalisation freedom remains:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      A_\mu\mapsto\lambda A_\mu,
      \qquad q\mapsto\frac{q}{\lambda}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Only a canonically normalised kinetic term and an independently selected smallest representation fix
      what number is called \(e\). Predicting \(\alpha_{\rm EM}=e^2/(4\pi\hbar c)\) also requires the scale at
      which the boundary value is set and the renormalisation-group flow to the measured scale.</p>
    <p>The RH archive contains circle symmetries, transforms and spectral operators, but no theorem selecting a
      unique gauge kinetic coefficient or charge lattice for the splice. A zeta regularisation may assign a
      finite value to an expression once the physical operator is known; it cannot choose the operator or its
      field normalisation in advance.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> the route from second-splice phase to \(U(1)\) remains plausible as a symmetry
      promotion, while the numerical coupling remains underdetermined. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Gate four</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-halo-radius">No independent \(r_0\) for the halo prediction</h2>
    <p>The most concrete splice consequence found so far begins by mapping levels to decimal radii,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      r_n=r_0\,10^n,
      \qquad
      \Delta\Phi_n=\kappa_{10}\delta\Pi_n.
    \]</p>
    <p>If the deposit per decade approaches \(L\), then</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Phi(r)\longrightarrow\frac{\kappa_{10}L}{\ln10}\ln\!\left(\frac r{r_0}\right),
      \qquad
      v_c^2\longrightarrow\frac{\kappa_{10}L}{\ln10},
      \qquad
      \rho(r)=\frac{v_\infty^2}{4\pi G r^2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The finite corrections predict an approach exponent
      \(p=\ln\varphi/\ln10\approx0.20899\) and a decaying decimal log-periodic residual. That is an
      interesting conditional signature. It becomes predictive only if \(r_0\) is fixed independently and the
      same source explains both dynamics and lensing.</p>
    <p>The RH archive contains logarithmic prime frequencies and zero ordinates, so it is tempting to identify
      one with \(r_0\). No unique dimensionally valid map does so. Choosing the first zero, a mean spacing or a
      prime scale because it improves a galaxy fit would introduce exactly the retrospective freedom the
      prediction is meant to remove. One universal anchor must be derived before looking at individual rotation
      curves, or tied to a separately measured formation variable by one preregistered law.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> no independent radius emerges from RH. The conditional flat-curve and
      log-periodic results remain intact, but not calibrated. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
