    <h2 id="t-rh-decimal-spectrum-test">The decimal-spectrum test</h2>
    <p>One genuinely useful synthesis is methodological. The splice predicts discrete scale structure; the RH
      work is expert at turning arithmetic sequences into spectra and at exposing how finite transforms can
      mislead. TTI can therefore formulate a preregistered decimal-spectrum test without claiming that zeta
      zeros cause galaxies.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Derive the residual shape from the exact \(\delta\Pi_n\), including both the monotone
        \(\varphi^{-n}\) term and the alternating \((-1)^n\varphi^{-2n-2}\) term.</li>
      <li>Map \(n=\log_{10}(r/r_0)\) with one independently specified rule for \(r_0\).</li>
      <li>Construct the predicted covariance in \(\log r\), including measurement windows and baryonic-model
        uncertainty.</li>
      <li>Fit a conventional smooth-halo null and the preregistered splice alternative to rotation and lensing
        data jointly.</li>
      <li>Penalise every free phase, anchor or object-specific amplitude. Reject the splice residual if its
        out-of-sample likelihood does not beat the null by the declared threshold.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>RH contributes no physical coefficient to this protocol. It contributes the insistence that a spectral
      pattern needs a convergence domain, an uncertainty budget and a universal quantifier stated honestly.</p>
